    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my realtor just mentioned something about an escrow account for the closing and i'm kinda confused. what exactly is escrow and why do i need it for the house purchase?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Great question — escrow confuses a lot of first-time buyers, and honestly, it's a completely normal thing to feel lost about. Let me break it down.\n\n**What escrow actually is:**\n\nEscrow is basically a neutral third party holding money (or documents) during a transaction until all the conditions of the deal are met. Think of it like a referee with a locked box. Neither you nor the seller can just grab the money and run — the escrow agent holds it safely until everyone has done their part.\n\n**Why you need it for a house purchase:**\n\n1. **Protection for both sides.** You don't want to hand over your down payment before you're sure the seller actually transfers the house, and the seller doesn't want to sign over the deed before they know your money is real. Escrow solves that standoff. The money sits in escrow while inspections, appraisals, and title checks happen — and it only gets released when the deal closes.\n\n2. **It's where your earnest money lives.** That good-faith deposit you put down when your offer was accepted? It goes into escrow, not straight into the seller's pocket. If the deal falls through for a valid",
      "source": "target",
