      "content": "Good follow-up — this is exactly the right thing to ask before closing. Here's how it works:\n\n**Who decides when money gets released:**\n\nThe escrow agent doesn't just sit on the money — they follow the **escrow instructions**, which are written documents both you and the seller signed. These spell out exactly what has to happen before funds are released. The agent is legally bound to follow those instructions, not to make judgment calls.\n\n**What happens in a dispute:**\n\n1. **First, the agent freezes.** If you and the seller disagree about whether conditions were met, the escrow agent won't release funds to either side. They just hold everything until the disagreement is resolved.\n\n2. **Then you negotiate.** Most disputes get worked out between the parties — often with realtors, attorneys, or mediators helping. If you and the seller agree on a resolution (say, a credit at closing instead of a repair), the escrow instructions get amended and things move forward.\n\n3. **If you can't agree, it can escalate.** Some escrow agreements include dispute-resolution clauses. In worst cases, someone files for **interpleader** — a legal action where the escrow holder asks a court",
